<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute periapical abscess, lower right first molar (K04.7)</content>. Localized dental abscess with swelling and a low-grade temperature, without signs of spreading infection or airway risk. Definitive treatment is dental drainage; an antibiotic is appropriate because of the swelling and temperature until the dentist can drain it. [S]<list><item>Amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily for seven days.</item><item>Dental appointment moved to this week by phone, with her agreement, for drainage or root canal treatment.</item><item>Go to the emergency department for trouble swallowing or breathing, swelling under the tongue or down the neck, or a high fever.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Opioid use disorder on buprenorphine: acute pain (F11.20)</content>. Acute pain in a person on buprenorphine is treated without stopping it. Buprenorphine continues at the same daily dose, and scheduled ibuprofen with acetaminophen relieves dental pain better than an opioid. No full-agonist opioid is needed. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm; naloxone and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline are in her phone and bag. [S]<list><item>Continue buprenorphine with naloxone 16 mg daily.</item><item>Ibuprofen 400 mg every 6 to 8 hours with food and acetaminophen 1,000 mg every 8 hours, no more than 3,000 mg a day because of her alcohol use, both for up to five days.</item><item>Her peer recovery coach and counselor told, with her consent, that she is in pain this week.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder on acamprosate (F10.20)</content>. No alcohol since the pain began; alcohol would add to the stomach risk of ibuprofen and the liver risk of acetaminophen. [S]<list><item>No alcohol while taking ibuprofen and acetaminophen; continue acamprosate.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: The ASAM National Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Opioid Use Disorder: 2020 Focused Update (2020); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); Evidence-based clinical practice guideline on antibiotic use for the urgent management of pulpal- and periapical-related dental pain and intraoral swelling (2019)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Dental drainage this week. Phone check in two days. Six-month recovery review as scheduled. Next scheduled visit 1948-10-19: six-month recovery, alcohol, liver, and mood review. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
